CAT = "aatl2CashLedgers"; SEG = "CSH"
Q = [
("A cash sale is paid in notes and coins and kept in the till. In a cash book with cash and bank columns, it is entered on the:",
 "Debit side, cash column", ["Credit side, cash column", "Debit side, bank column", "Credit side, bank column"],
 "Money received goes on the debit side of the cash book. It goes in the cash column because it has not been paid into the bank."),
("A café keeps a petty cash imprest of £300. At the end of the week the tin holds £82.35 and vouchers for £217.65. To restore the imprest, the cashier draws:",
 "£217.65", ["£82.35", "£300.00", "£517.65"],
 "Under the imprest system, the float is topped up by exactly what was spent, £217.65, bringing it back to £300. £82.35 is the cash still in the tin."),
("Under the imprest system, the petty cash float at the start of each period is:",
 "Restored to a fixed amount", ["Whatever cash was left over", "Topped up by a fixed sum", "Paid into the bank account"],
 "The imprest is restored to the same agreed amount each period, so any shortage is easy to spot. Topping up by a fixed sum is a non-imprest system."),
("A petty cash voucher is for £27.60 including VAT at 20%. The amount entered in the VAT column is:",
 "£4.60", ["£5.52", "£23.00", "£2.76"],
 "VAT = £27.60 x 20/120 = £4.60, and the net expense is £23.00. Taking 20% of the gross amount gives £5.52."),
("A non-imprest petty cash float starts the week at £200. Vouchers total £60, then a fixed £150 is added. The float is now:",
 "£290", ["£200", "£350", "£150"],
 "£200 - £60 + £150 = £290. Under a non-imprest system the top-up does not match spending, so the float drifts. £350 forgets the vouchers."),
("The bank columns of the cash book total £18,300 on the debit side and £21,050 on the credit side. The balance brought down is:",
 "£2,750 credit", ["£2,750 debit", "£39,350 debit", "£3,750 credit"],
 "Payments exceed receipts by £21,050 - £18,300 = £2,750. A credit balance brought down on the bank column means the account is overdrawn."),
("When balancing a ledger account, the balance carried down is written on the:",
 "Side with the lower total", ["Side with the higher total", "Debit side always", "Credit side always"],
 "The balance carried down makes the smaller side equal the larger. It is then brought down on the larger side, which shows whether the balance is debit or credit."),
("A credit customer's cheque for £1,370 is banked. The cash book is part of the double-entry system. Which general ledger entry completes it?",
 "Credit receivables ledger control", ["Debit the receivables ledger control", "Credit sales", "Debit sales"],
 "The cash book debit is the bank entry. The customer owes less, so the receivables ledger control account is credited. The sale was recorded when it was invoiced."),
("A cash payment of £96 including VAT at 20% is for stationery. In the analysed cash book, the net stationery figure is:",
 "£80.00", ["£76.80", "£96.00", "£16.00"],
 "Net = £96 / 1.2 = £80, and VAT is £16. Taking 20% off the gross gives £76.80, which is wrong because the VAT was added to the net, not the gross."),
("Which document supports cash and cheques paid into the bank over the counter?",
 "Paying-in slip", ["Cheque stub", "Remittance advice", "Petty cash voucher"],
 "A paying-in slip lists what is being deposited and is stamped by the bank. A cheque stub is the record of a cheque the business wrote."),
("Which document is the business's own record of a cheque it has written?",
 "Cheque stub", ["Paying-in slip", "Bank statement", "Cash receipt"],
 "The cheque stub (counterfoil) stays in the chequebook and shows the date, payee and amount, so the payment can be entered in the cash book."),
("The cash book is used as a book of prime entry only. Its bank receipts total must then also be posted to the:",
 "Bank account in the general ledger", ["Receivables ledger only", "Petty cash book", "Sales daybook"],
 "When the cash book is only a book of prime entry, the double entry happens in the general ledger, so the totals are posted to a separate bank account there."),
("A supplier's account in the payables ledger has credits of £6,380 and debits of £5,915. The balance brought down is:",
 "£465 credit", ["£465 debit", "£12,295 credit", "£565 credit"],
 "Credits exceed debits by £6,380 - £5,915 = £465, so the balance brought down is £465 credit: the business still owes the supplier £465."),
("Sales returns daybook totals are: net £640, VAT £128, total £768. Which entry posts the VAT column?",
 "Debit VAT £128", ["Credit VAT £128", "Debit VAT £768", "Credit sales returns £128"],
 "Returns cancel part of the VAT charged on sales, so VAT is debited with £128. Sales returns are debited with £640 and the receivables ledger control credited with £768."),
("Discounts allowed daybook totals: net £90, VAT £18, total £108. Which entry posts the total column?",
 "Credit receivables control £108", ["Debit receivables control £108", "Credit the discounts allowed £108", "Debit discounts allowed £108"],
 "The discount reduces what customers owe, so the receivables ledger control account is credited with £108. Discounts allowed (£90) and VAT (£18) are debited."),
("A business pays a supplier £2,160 by BACS. How is this recorded in the payables ledger?",
 "Debit the supplier's account", ["Credit the supplier's account", "Debit the purchases account", "Credit the bank account"],
 "Paying a supplier reduces what the business owes, so the supplier's account is debited. Purchases and bank are general ledger accounts, not payables ledger accounts."),
]
